    "innovation": "This paper uses a dynamic global vegetation model to assess trade-offs between livestock productivity and environmental impacts in the dry steppes of Russia and Kazakhstan, demonstrating that moderately intensive management can substantially reduce environmental harm while maintaining high production.",
    "kg_extraction_triplets": [
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "LPJmL",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_SOURCE_COMPONENT",
            "target_entity_instance": "grazing component",
            "target_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "paragraph": "In this study, the focus is on grassland dynamics under grazing management, where feed quantity (growing above-ground herbaceous vegetation) and quality (protein content, which is approximated from floating C:N ratios) affect livestock productivity. At the same time, livestock stocking densities and feed quality affect the grazed biomass and growth of the herbaceous vegetation. Nitrogen from manure during grazing is directly returned to the soil and constitutes a fertilizer for plant growth, especially in low-input systems. Nitrogen in the soil is subject to water-, material- and temperature-dependent turnover rates. The complete complexity of the grazing-plant growth interaction is described in detail by Heinke et al. (2023).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "LPJmL",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_SOURCE_COMPONENT",
            "target_entity_instance": "soil component",
            "target_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "paragraph": "Carbon (C), water, and nitrogen (N) dynamics are tightly coupled in LPJmL through modeled stomatal conductance and N-dependent carboxylation capacity in the photosynthesis and growth modules as well as in soils. Vegetation is represented as plant functional types (PFTs) with three different herbaceous PFTs and eight tree PFTs. Land use is prescribed per fractions of total land in each simulated grid cell.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "grazing component",
            "source_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "relation": "PRODUCES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "methane (CH4)",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "Carbon intake is converted to yield, to GHG emissions in the form of CO2 and methane (CH4) and excreta in the form of feces and urine. Nitrogen intake is converted to yield and excreta in the form of feces and urine. Excreta are subject to further decomposition and mineralization, leading to additional GHG emissions of CO2, N2O, and NH4 as well as to the supply of reactive nitrogen forms for plant growth (von Bloh et al., 2018; Heinke et al., 2023). For this study, we use LPJmL version 5.6.18 (Schaphoff et al., 2026).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
